// =============================================================================
// AI-103 Lesson 11 -- Query A: agent KPIs
//
// "How is each agent performing?" This is the first question you answer in an
// agent incident review: which agent is slow, which is failing, how often each
// one runs.
//
// Requires spans from m11_multi_agent.py (or generate_traffic.py). If this
// returns nothing, the telemetry pipe is cold -- run a demo first, then allow
// 1-3 minutes for ingestion.
//
// Paste into: Application Insights -> Logs
// =============================================================================
dependencies
| where timestamp > ago(24h)
| where name startswith "invoke_agent" or name startswith "executor.process"
// The agent name is the second token: "invoke_agent billing" -> "billing".
| extend agent = tostring(split(name, " ")[1])
| where isnotempty(agent)
// A handoff workflow polls every participant on each superstep, and the idle
// ones complete in ~0 ms. Including those would drag p50 to zero and make a
// working agent look instant, so latency is measured over spans that did work.
// A handoff workflow polls every participant on each superstep, and the idle
// ones complete in ~0 ms. Including those would drag p50 to zero and make a
// working agent look instant, so latency is measured only over spans that did
// work. `calls` still counts every invocation, so you can see both numbers.
| extend work_ms = iff(duration > 1, duration, real(null))
| summarize
    calls        = count(),
    working      = countif(duration > 1),
    failures     = countif(success == false),
    p50_ms       = round(percentile(work_ms, 50), 1),
    p95_ms       = round(percentile(work_ms, 95), 1),
    last_seen    = max(timestamp)
    by agent
| extend failure_rate = round(100.0 * failures / calls, 2)
| order by calls desc
